2708: build the onboard Bluetooth transport

AROS has a Bluetooth stack, two firmware loaders and an HCI device -- and the
HCI device is vbthci, which is virtual. On real hardware the stack has
nothing to talk to. This wires up the three modules that give it the
Raspberry Pi's onboard controller:

  pl011bt.resource   the PL011 the BCM283x wires to the radio: the pin mux,
                     the 32.768 kHz LPO on GPCLK2, BT_REG_EN through the
                     firmware's GPIO expander, the baud divisor and the
                     receive interrupt
  h4bthci.device     H:4 framing over that transport, in DEVS:Bluetooth
                     beside vbthci
  brcmbt.fwl         the Broadcom patchram loader, in DEVS:Bluetooth/FWLoaders
                     beside rtlbtv1 and rtlbtv2. A BCM43438 runs from ROM with
                     no address of its own until it is sent one

Both packages that carry the bcm2708 drivers get all three, and pl011bt joins
the resources in the ROM image the way mbox and sdio do. The device and the
loader stay files on disk, because that is where BTStackLoader and
AddBTHardware look for them.

